  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>With severe head injury, an extensive stroke or marked brain swelling, surgeons sometimes remove part of the skull bone — a <strong>decompressive craniectomy</strong>. It saves lives: the swollen brain gets room and does not compress itself. Bone is also removed if it is shattered or affected by a tumour or infection.</p>
    <p>This leaves a <strong>skull defect</strong> — an area where the brain is protected only by skin and soft tissue. Sometimes it reaches 10–12 centimetres. <strong>Cranioplasty</strong> is the operation that closes this defect.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="zachem">
    <h2>Why close the defect</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain protection.</strong> Without bone, any blow or fall on this area is dangerous.</li>
      <li><strong>Wellbeing.</strong> Many patients with a large defect have headaches, dizziness, fatigue and weather sensitivity. After cranioplasty these complaints often decrease.</li>
      <li><strong>Recovery.</strong> Closing the defect helps normalise blood flow and fluid circulation in the brain, which matters for rehabilitation.</li>
      <li><strong>Appearance.</strong> The natural shape of the head returns, and with it self-confidence.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="materialy">
    <h2>What the implant is made of</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The patient's own bone</strong>, stored after the first operation. Sometimes it can be put back, but over time it may partly resorb.</li>
      <li><strong>A custom titanium implant.</strong> A 3D model of the skull is built from CT data and an implant is made that exactly follows its shape. Titanium is strong, light and well tolerated by the body.</li>
      <li>Other materials — chosen individually.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I most often use custom titanium implants: they fit the defect precisely and give a predictable result even with very large defects.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>Surgery</h2>
    <p>The operation is done under general anaesthesia. The incision is usually made along the old scar. The surgeon frees the edges of the defect, places the implant and fixes it to the bone edges with micro-screws. A temporary drain may stay for the first day. The hospital stay after surgery is about a week on average.</p>
